  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta ={sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta ={sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta -{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta +1}=\\frac{{sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta +1}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify our expression by factoring the numerator as a difference of squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta +1} & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta -1)(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta +1)}{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta +1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta -1)\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta +1)}}{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta +1)}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta +1}=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta -1.\n$$",
